## Key risks and safeguards

| Risk | Safeguard |
|---|---|
| Innovation becomes a slogan | Provide time, funding, decision rights, and executive accountability |
| Employees fear career damage from failure | Define and protect intelligent failure; monitor retaliation |
| Local cultural norms inhibit public dissent | Use private, written, facilitated, and anonymous participation routes |
| Headquarters imposes one method everywhere | Keep global principles but co-design local practices |
| Idea portals generate volume but no action | Assign owners, response deadlines, feedback, and funding paths |
| Managers suppress employee ideas | Measure manager behavior and provide escalation routes |
| Teams bypass compliance in the name of speed | Use risk-tiered reviews and involve control functions early |
| Rewards create competition or gaming | Reward learning, collaboration, adoption, and value—not raw idea counts |
| Only fluent corporate-language speakers participate | Support local languages and multiple submission formats |
| Successful ideas remain local | Assign replication ownership and reward adoption by other offices |
| Innovation adds to existing workload | Protect time and adjust operating targets |
| Failed projects continue because of executive attachment | Establish evidence-based stop criteria and independent portfolio reviews |

## Bottom line

A more innovative international company requires more than creativity workshops or an idea platform. Employees must see that leadership welcomes challenge, small experiments can be approved quickly, responsible failure does not damage careers, and useful ideas receive resources and recognition.

The strongest model combines **global strategic direction and ethical boundaries with locally adapted participation methods**. It makes innovation part of management behavior, resource allocation, career development, and everyday problem-solving—while using disciplined experiments to ensure that greater risk-taking produces learning and business value rather than uncontrolled exposure.
